    "pros": [
      "Stocked by most landscape suppliers and many big-box stores",
      "Forgiving of small watering mistakes compared with older formulas",
      "Several colors including beige, slate grey and black",
      "Firm set that resists washout on gently sloped patios",
      "Clear instructions printed on the bag"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Built for paver joints, not wide flagstone gaps",
      "Still needs bone-dry pavers and a dry forecast",
      "Can haze if dust is left on textured surfaces"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The default choice for a standard concrete or clay paver patio when you want a proven sand you can buy locally."
  },
  {
    "badge": "Best Haze-Resistant",
    "name": "Techniseal HP NextGel Polymeric Sand",
    "category": "Gel-Activated Paver Sand",
    "id": "techniseal-hp-nextgel",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "description": "Techniseal built the NextGel line around the most common complaint about polymeric sand: a cloudy polymer film left on the paver surface after watering. Instead of relying on a binder that dries as a residue wherever water carries it, the formula is designed to gel inside the joint, which reduces how much binder ends up smeared across the paver faces. That matters most on smooth, light-colored or already-sealed pavers, where haze shows plainly and stripping it means another product and another weekend. Techniseal also promotes the line as more tolerant of rain soon after installation than older sands; read the current bag for the exact window instead of assuming one. It is aimed at concrete pavers and some natural stone with joints in the paver range. Pricing runs roughly $35-$55 per bag at the time of writing, near the top of the market. Owners commonly describe the cured joint as hard on top with a touch of give, which helps on bases that shift slightly between seasons.",
    "pros": [
      "Marketed specifically around low-haze installation",
      "Polymer forms a gel in the joint rather than a film on paver faces",
      "Good choice for smooth, light or pre-sealed pavers",
      "Firm but slightly flexible joint once cured",
      "Available in several colors"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Usually priced at the top of the category",
      "Narrower retail availability than Gator in some regions",
      "Not designed for very wide natural stone joints"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Worth the premium on smooth or light-colored pavers where a polymer haze would be obvious and hard to remove."
  },
  {
    "badge": "Best for Flagstone",
    "name": "Alliance GatorDust Stone Dust Binder",
    "category": "Wide-Joint Stone Binder",
    "id": "alliance-gatordust",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "description": "GatorDust is a different animal from fine paver sand. It is a coarser, stone-dust style binder that Alliance designs for flagstone, natural stone and other wide-joint work, and its label range extends to several inches of joint width rather than fractions of an inch. That coarser particle mix is what lets a wide joint stay filled and locked: fine paver sands asked to bridge a 2-inch gap tend to shrink, crack along the center line and wash out after a few storms. The cured surface is firm but a little softer than paver sand, which suits irregular flagging set on a compacted aggregate base that moves slightly with the seasons. Colors are limited to a handful of earth tones that read naturally against stone. Budget carefully: a wide, deep joint swallows material, so one 50 lb bag covers a fraction of the area it would on a paver patio. Pricing is roughly $30-$45 per bag at the time of writing. Do not try to use it on narrow concrete paver joints.",
    "pros": [
      "Coarse gradation made to bridge wide flagstone joints",
      "Label range runs into multiple inches of joint width",
      "Slightly softer set tolerates seasonal movement in flagging",
      "Natural earth tones blend with stone"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Will not pack into tight paver joints",
      "Wide joints consume bags quickly",
      "Fewer color choices than paver sands"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The pick for irregular flagstone, bluestone and fieldstone laid with joints too wide for any paver sand."
  },
