          position: 3,
          name: "Best Polymeric Sand",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-polymeric-sand/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which polymeric sand is best for flagstone?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For flagstone and other natural stone with wide, irregular joints, choose a wide-joint binder rather than paver sand. Alliance GatorDust and Techniseal RG+ are both designed for joints running into several inches. They use a coarser grain that bridges wide gaps without cracking down the middle. Check the label for the maximum width and minimum depth, and buy more bags than you would for pavers, because wide joints use material quickly."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use paver polymeric sand in wide joints?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only up to the maximum width on the bag, which for most fine paver sands is around 1/2 inch to 1 inch. Beyond that, a fine sand tends to shrink, crack along the center and wash out. If your joints vary, use paver sand for the narrow sections and a wide-joint binder for the rest, and keep the two areas visually separated, such as by a border."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How deep should polymeric sand joints be?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most manufacturers want the polymeric layer at least 1 to 1.5 inches deep, and many recommend filling to the full depth of the paver. Shallower joints do not have enough material for the binder to hold, so they crack and lift. Clean out old sand and debris down to that depth before installing, and stop the fill about 1/8 inch below the paver surface or chamfer."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is polymeric sand safe for pets and gardens?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Once cured, polymeric sand is inert and generally considered safe for pets and nearby plants. The risk is during installation: the dry product contains crystalline silica, which you should not breathe, so wear an N95 mask and keep pets and children away while pouring and sweeping. Rinse runoff away from vegetable beds as a precaution, and follow the label on the bag."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does polymeric sand stop all weeds?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. It stops weeds rooting through the joint from below and makes the joint too hard for most seeds to establish in. But wind-blown seeds can germinate in dust and organic debris that collects on top of the joint. Regular sweeping keeps this to a minimum. If weeds are a serious problem, it is far better than plain sand, but not maintenance free."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does polymeric sand work with porcelain pavers?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It can, but take care. Porcelain absorbs almost nothing, so any binder left on the surface stays visible as haze. Choose a low-haze formula, blow the surface completely clean, and mist lightly. Some manufacturers make jointing products specifically for porcelain, which are worth considering. Check that your joint width and depth meet the sand's minimums, since porcelain is often installed with thinner profiles."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many years will a polymeric joint hold up?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A properly installed polymeric joint commonly lasts three to ten years before it needs refreshing, sometimes longer on sheltered patios with light traffic. Lifespan drops with poor installation, unstable bases, aggressive pressure washing and heavy freeze-thaw cycles. When joints drop below the paver edge or start to crumble, remove the old sand from the affected areas and reinstall rather than topping up."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
