        <div id="how-it-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Polymeric Sand Actually Does in a Joint
          </h2>

          <p>
            Polymeric sand is graded sand blended with a dry polymer binder. On its own, sand in a paver joint is held only by friction and gravity, so rain, hose spray and leaf blowers slowly carry it away. Add water to polymeric sand and the binder activates, coating the grains and gluing them to each other as it dries. The result is a joint that behaves like firm, slightly flexible mortar rather than loose fill.
          </p>

          <p>
            That firmness does three useful jobs. It keeps the joint full, which keeps the pavers interlocked so they do not rock or shift under foot traffic. It denies weed seeds and ants an easy place to settle, because a hardened joint is hard to dig into and offers little loose soil. And it stops sand from washing onto the surface, which is the main reason plain-sand patios look patchy after a couple of seasons.
          </p>

          <p>
            What polymeric sand does not do is waterproof anything. Water still passes through and around the joint into the bedding layer, and it should: a patio needs that drainage. Nor is it a herbicide. Wind-blown seeds can still germinate in organic debris that collects on top of a joint, so expect to sweep occasionally. Treat the product as a joint stabilizer, not a sealant and not a weed killer.
          </p>

          <p>
            The chemistry also explains why installation is unforgiving. The binder starts working the moment it meets moisture, so damp pavers activate it on the surface where you do not want it, and too much water can flush binder out of the top of the joint before it sets. Nearly every failure covered later on this page traces back to water arriving at the wrong time, in the wrong amount, or in the wrong place.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="joint-width" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Match the Sand to the Joint Width First
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before comparing brands, take a tape measure to your joints. Measure the narrowest and widest gaps across the area, and check depth by pushing a screwdriver down until it hits bedding sand or base. Every bag lists a joint width range and usually a minimum depth. Buying outside that range is the single most common reason a polymeric sand job fails within a year.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concrete pavers laid with spacer nubs typically have joints in the 1/16 to 3/16 inch range, which suits fine paver sands like Gator Maxx G2, HP NextGel and PowerLoc. Tumbled or antique-style pavers often run wider, closer to 1/4 to 1/2 inch, which most paver sands still cover. Flagstone, bluestone and fieldstone laid with irregular gaps can run from 1/2 inch to 3 inches or more, and that is wide-joint binder territory.
          </p>

